INT. SALT MINE ELEVATOR SHAFT - DEEP LEVEL - NIGHT

A cage of dented iron wedged halfway between two galleries. Above, a ceiling of glittering rock salt, pink and white in the glow of a single miner's lamp. The air is dry and tastes of old tears. Somewhere far below, a pump shudders.

CALLISTA REYNOLDS-OKAFOR (30s, hair cropped, climbing gloves, a dusty duffel slung across her chest) crouches on the floor of the cage, a small drill whining in her hands.

On the opposite side, JONAH "SPARROW" PELLEGRINI (60s, wiry, a lifetime of tunnels written into his knees) sits with his back to the bars, a radio resting on his thigh.

The radio crackles.

DISPATCH (V.O.)
(filtered, distant)
Cage Four, Cage Four, this is surface. Do you copy?

Sparrow lifts the radio and looks at Callista. She shakes her head, once.

SPARROW
(into radio)
Surface, Cage Four. We copy. Slight delay on the cable.

DISPATCH (V.O.)
Roger. We show you stopped at minus three hundred. Any injuries?

SPARROW
None. Cable guide's jammed. Giving it ten.

DISPATCH (V.O.)
Ten minutes, copy. Safety crew standing by.

Sparrow lowers the radio. The drill falls silent. Callista rests her palms on the floor, listening to the pump.

CALLISTA
Ten minutes.

SPARROW
Twelve, if I stall right.

CALLISTA
We need twenty.

SPARROW
Then you shouldn't have asked for the cage.

CALLISTA
I asked for the cage because you said it stopped here every night.

SPARROW
It does. For nine minutes. Then the night foreman rides the other one.

CALLISTA
You didn't tell me about the foreman.

SPARROW
You didn't ask.

She stands, shoulders the duffel. Moves to the wall of the shaft, where a seam of white salt runs like a vein of frost. She sets the drill against it. The whine resumes.

A second voice, a woman's, cuts through the static.

DISPATCH (V.O.)
(from far above, echoing down the shaft)
Cage Four! Sparrow! What are you doing down there?

Callista and Sparrow both go still. The drill spins down. Salt crystals tick onto the floor.

SPARROW
(calling up)
Cable jam, Yvonne! Nothing to see!

DISPATCH (V.O.)
I'm coming down the ladder. Don't move!

Sparrow looks at Callista. She looks at the duffel, then up the shaft, where a lamp has begun to swing.

CALLISTA
(quiet)
Don't you dare call me a friend of yours.

SPARROW
Wouldn't dream of it.

He sets the radio down, stands, and with a grunt drags the duffel to his side of the cage. He sits on it.

Callista understands. She steps out of the lamplight, into the pink shadow of the wall.

The lamp above steadies. A boot appears on the rungs.
